course: dictionary
lesson: 5806
title: Magic in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word magic has several meanings, and the words around it tell you which one you need. «But when the Magician asks for a Magic, Pau Amma can only stir up the sea.» Here magic means a special power to make impossible things happen, like the spells in a fairy tale.
explain: Look for words that change the meaning. «Runes were mostly writing, not magic spells.» The word not tells you that runes are not spells, so magic spells are things you might expect runes to be. The sentence is saying that runes are something else.
explain: Magic can also mean a special, wonderful quality, as in the magic of Christmas. Try the swap test: put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense. If it does, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Magic can mean a special power to make impossible things happen, as in stories and fairy tales.
fact: A magic trick is an illusion: the magician uses skill and hiding things to make something look impossible.
fact: Magic can mean a special, wonderful quality that makes something exciting, as in the magic of Christmas.
fact: Informal UK: «That's magic!» means that is great or excellent.
fact: In children's talk, the magic words are «please» and «thank you».
exercise: «But when the Magician asks for a Magic, Pau Amma can only stir up the sea.» What does magic mean here? | Tiny and quiet | *A special power to make impossible things happen | Very cold | Written in runes
exercise: Ella says «That's magic!» about her new bike. What does she mean? | It needs a new battery | *It is great and excellent | It is broken | It is very ordinary
exercise: «Runes were mostly writing, not magic spells.» Which word shows that runes were not spells? | were | *not | mostly | writing
test: What does the magic of Christmas mean? | Snow falling at night | *A special, wonderful quality that makes it exciting | The shops are open late | A kind of fizzy drink
test: A magic trick at a show looks impossible. What is it really? | A fairy's gift | A card game for two | *An illusion, done with skill and hiding things | A real spell from a wizard
exam: A child says «please» is a magic word. What does she mean? | *Polite words that children use | A word from a spell book | Only magicians may say it | It makes impossible things happen
